            <ArticleTitle>Substance P suppresses stress-induced eating.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>309-11</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Parenterally administered substance P suppressed stress-induced eating (resulting from mild tail pinch) in a dose-related manner, whereas at similar or higher doses of substance P starvation-induced eating was uneffected. This specific effect of substance P on stress-induced eating is possibly associated with alterations of exogenous substance P of the normal modulation of pain transmission.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
